{"declarations":[{"header":"<div class=\"decl_header\"><span class=\"decl_kind\">def</span><span class=\"decl_name\"> <a class=\"break_within\" href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.maximumNumber\"><span class=\"name\">Float</span>.<span class=\"name\">Model</span>.<span class=\"name\">UnpackedFloat</span>.<span class=\"name\">maximumNumber</span></a> </span><span class=\"decl_args\"> :</span><div class=\"decl_type\"><span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a></span></div></div>","info":{"doc":"Computes the IEEE-754-2019 `maximumNumber` of two floats. A `NaN` operand is ignored in favor of\nthe other operand, so the result is `NaN` only if both operands are `NaN`; otherwise it is the\nlarger operand, where `-0` is considered smaller than `+0`.\n\nImportant: this operation only works correctly if the two inputs are in\ncanonical form for a common format (see the docstring for `UnpackedFloat` for details.)","docLink":"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.maximumNumber","kind":"def","line":60,"name":"Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.maximumNumber","sourceLink":"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/c9afcddf514a6772bd4d649f7cfc04eff0b9f15e/src/Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.lean#L60-L73"}},{"header":"<div class=\"decl_header\"><span class=\"decl_kind\">def</span><span class=\"decl_name\"> <a class=\"break_within\" href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.maximum\"><span class=\"name\">Float</span>.<span class=\"name\">Model</span>.<span class=\"name\">UnpackedFloat</span>.<span class=\"name\">maximum</span></a> </span><span class=\"decl_args\"> :</span><div class=\"decl_type\"><span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a></span></div></div>","info":{"doc":"Computes the IEEE-754-2019 `maximum` of two floats. The result is `NaN` if either operand is\n`NaN`; otherwise it is the larger operand, where `-0` is considered smaller than `+0`.\n\nImportant: this operation only works correctly if the two inputs are in\ncanonical form for a common format (see the docstring for `UnpackedFloat` for details.)","docLink":"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.maximum","kind":"def","line":46,"name":"Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.maximum","sourceLink":"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/c9afcddf514a6772bd4d649f7cfc04eff0b9f15e/src/Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.lean#L46-L58"}},{"header":"<div class=\"decl_header\"><span class=\"decl_kind\">def</span><span class=\"decl_name\"> <a class=\"break_within\" href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.minimumNumber\"><span class=\"name\">Float</span>.<span class=\"name\">Model</span>.<span class=\"name\">UnpackedFloat</span>.<span class=\"name\">minimumNumber</span></a> </span><span class=\"decl_args\"> :</span><div class=\"decl_type\"><span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a></span></div></div>","info":{"doc":"Computes the IEEE-754-2019 `minimumNumber` of two floats. A `NaN` operand is ignored in favor of\nthe other operand, so the result is `NaN` only if both operands are `NaN`; otherwise it is the\nsmaller operand, where `-0` is considered smaller than `+0`.\n\nImportant: this operation only works correctly if the two inputs are in\ncanonical form for a common format (see the docstring for `UnpackedFloat` for details.)","docLink":"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.minimumNumber","kind":"def","line":31,"name":"Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.minimumNumber","sourceLink":"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/c9afcddf514a6772bd4d649f7cfc04eff0b9f15e/src/Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.lean#L31-L44"}},{"header":"<div class=\"decl_header\"><span class=\"decl_kind\">def</span><span class=\"decl_name\"> <a class=\"break_within\" href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.minimum\"><span class=\"name\">Float</span>.<span class=\"name\">Model</span>.<span class=\"name\">UnpackedFloat</span>.<span class=\"name\">minimum</span></a> </span><span class=\"decl_args\"> :</span><div class=\"decl_type\"><span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Basic.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat\">UnpackedFloat</a></span></div></div>","info":{"doc":"Computes the IEEE-754-2019 `minimum` of two floats. The result is `NaN` if either operand is\n`NaN`; otherwise it is the smaller operand, where `-0` is considered smaller than `+0`.\n\nImportant: this operation only works correctly if the two inputs are in\ncanonical form for a common format (see the docstring for `UnpackedFloat` for details.)","docLink":"./Init/Data/Float/Model/Unpacked/Operations/MinMax.html#Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.minimum","kind":"def","line":17,"name":"Float.Model.UnpackedFloat.minimum","sourceLink":"https://github.com